--TEST--
A member that waits parks only the calling task: a sibling runs while it sleeps and resumes first, each task's own statements keep their order, and the waiting call answers its value with nothing to unwrap — `rule:concurrency/one-scheduler`, `rule:concurrency/a-child-belongs-to-the-calling-task`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:concurrency/no-colouring-i-o-just-waits
// The trace is a static string and not `echo` on purpose: output written inside a
// task is collected and emitted one whole task at a time, so an `echo` trace reads
// `long, long, short, short` whatever the two tasks actually did. This is the
// discriminator for the whole no-colouring claim — a `sleep` that parked the
// scheduler rather than the task would order the trace the same way `echo` does.
class Trace {
    public static string $steps = "";

    public static function note(string $what): void
    {
        Trace::$steps = Trace::$steps . $what . "\n";
    }
}

class Slow {
    // An ordinary static method. Nothing about it is coloured by the wait inside it:
    // no `async`, and no promise at the call site below.
    public static function value(): int
    {
        Core\Time::sleep(5ms);
        return 7;
    }
}

Core\Task::all({
    long: fn(): void => {
        Trace::note("long starts");
        Core\Time::sleep(40ms);
        Trace::note("long wakes");
    },
    short: fn(): void => {
        Trace::note("short starts");
        Core\Time::sleep(5ms);
        Trace::note("short wakes");
    },
});

// Both tasks start before either one wakes, and `short` — which waits for an eighth
// of the time — wakes first, so the sleep in `long` parked that one task and nothing
// else. Each task's own two lines keep their order, which is the sequential half:
// neither task ran its second statement before its first.
echo Trace::$steps;

// The value half: `$n` is an `int` here and not a handle, so it is multiplied in the
// same expression with no `await` and no callback.
int $n = Slow::value();
echo "a waiting call answers a value: ", $n * 2, "\n";
--EXPECT--
long starts
short starts
short wakes
long wakes
a waiting call answers a value: 14
